    {
      "heading": "Frequently asked Questions",
      "group": "supporting-content",
      "text": "Frequently asked Questions What is a comforter? A comforter is a thick quilted blanket usually filled with synthetic fibre or cotton to give warmth. Is comforter same as blanket? No, a blanket is made from a single layer of fabric whereas a comforter is made from two layers of fabrics stitched together. What is a comforter vs duvet? Comforters are usually quilted or stitched and they do not need an additional cover. A duvet however, is meant to be used with a duvet cover. They are generally warmer than a comforter and easier to clean. Why is a comforter used? A comforter is a type of blanket used to keep one warm during sleep. Can you sleep with just a comforter? Yes, you may sleep with just a comforter. It will work just like a quilt or a blanket and help you stay warm in bed. Can you wash a comforter? Yes, you can safely wash a comforter at home if the care label instructions of the product specifiy so. Can I use a comforter without a duvet blanket cover? While duvet blanket covers help protect and preserve comforters, they are not necessary for use. However, using a duvet cover makes cleaning and changing the comforter easier and extends its lifespan. Are comforters suitable for all seasons? Comforters come in various thicknesses and warmth levels, making them suitable for different seasons. Lighter options are ideal for warmer climates, while heavier ones offer warmth during colder months. How do I clean and maintain my comforter? Always check the care instructions provided by the manufacturer. Many comforters are machine-washable on a gentle cycle, while some may require professional cleaning. . Can I use a comforter as a standalone blanket? Yes, comforters can be used as standalone blankets, providing warmth and comfort without the need for additional bedding layers. Are comforters suitable for children? Comforters are suitable for children and adults alike. However, it's essential to choose an age-appropriate size and avoid loose bedding for infants to reduce the risk of suffocation."
